Recent advancements in Session-based recommendation systems have led to the development of robust models capable of generating relevant recommendations solely based on users' past experiences. These models inherently account for users' transitions between items, making them well-suited for dynamic and personalized recommendations. One key challenge, however, lies in effectively leveraging the vast potential for users' future interactions. Addressing this issue, a novel approach using Candidate Rank Embedding (CARE) is introduced, allowing existing items to influence each other's recommendation probabilities without the need for explicit user feedback. By integrating CARE into a standard Graph Attention Network (GAT) framework, a two-stage recommendation system known as CARE-GAT is developed, incorporating both item-based recommendation and an optional user-mapping layer for enhancing diversity. Experimental evaluations on public datasets demonstrate the efficacy of CARE-GAT in surpassing state-of-the-art benchmarks for item-based recommendation and showcasing promising results for diversity enhancement. Additionally, a comprehensive analysis is provided to shed light on the underlying dynamics of the proposed model. Care-GAT source code will be publicly available. 

